ACC-022 — Ontology Specification

Document ID: ACC-022
Title: Ontology Specification
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical ontology of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The ontology formally establishes the concepts, semantic relationships, and governing rules that define engineering cognition within Atlas. It serves as the authoritative conceptual model for all specifications, implementations, APIs, knowledge graphs, reasoning engines, and software components.

2. Scope

This specification applies to all canonical concepts defined by the Atlas Core Concepts (ACC).

It defines:

Ontological entities
Semantic relationships
Dependency rules
Hierarchical organization
Concept integrity
Ontological conformance

This specification is independent of implementation technologies.

3. Canonical Definition

The Atlas Ontology is the formal semantic model that defines the identity, meaning, relationships, constraints, and organization of the canonical concepts comprising the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.

The ontology defines what exists, how concepts relate, and what relationships are valid.

4. Ontological Principles
4.1 Canonical Identity

Every ontology entity SHALL have one unique identity.

4.2 Canonical Meaning

Every entity SHALL possess one authoritative definition.

4.3 Explicit Semantics

Relationships between entities SHALL be explicitly defined.

4.4 Consistency

The ontology SHALL remain logically consistent.

Contradictory concepts SHALL NOT exist.

4.5 Traceability

Every entity SHALL be traceable to its canonical definition.

4.6 Evolvability

The ontology MAY evolve through controlled versioning while preserving compatibility where practical.

5. Ontological Layers

The Atlas ontology consists of five semantic layers.

Layer 1 — Reality
Engineering Reality

Represents the external engineering domain.

Layer 2 — Perception
Engineering Observation
Engineering Evidence

Represents acquisition and validation of engineering information.

Layer 3 — Cognition
Engineering Understanding
Engineering Memory
Engineering Knowledge
Engineering Reasoning

Represents internal engineering cognition.

Layer 4 — Execution
Engineering Capability
Decision Support

Represents engineering actions and outputs.

Layer 5 — Governance
Engineering Context
Engineering Intent
Engineering Constraints

Engineering Confidence
Engineering Traceability
Engineering Explainability

Represents governance and quality attributes that apply across all layers.

6. Ontological Relationships

The ontology recognizes the following canonical relationship types.

Relationship	Meaning
observes	acquires information from
validates	transforms observations into evidence
constructs	builds understanding
preserves	stores understanding over time
generalizes	derives reusable knowledge
reasons_over	analyzes engineering cognition
executes	performs engineering capabilities
supports	assists engineering decisions
governs	defines operational boundaries
qualifies	describes quality attributes
traces	maintains provenance
explains	communicates rationale
