8. Ontological Constraints

The following constraints SHALL apply.

Identity

A canonical concept SHALL appear exactly once within the ontology.

Definition

A canonical concept SHALL have exactly one canonical definition.

Relationships

Relationships SHALL conform to the canonical relationship types defined by this specification.

Dependencies

A concept SHALL NOT depend upon a concept positioned later in the cognitive pipeline unless explicitly specified.

Cycles

Circular dependencies SHOULD be avoided except where required to represent lifecycle feedback.

9. Conformance

An implementation conforms to the Atlas Ontology if it:

Preserves canonical concepts.
Preserves canonical definitions.
Preserves canonical relationships.
Preserves dependency rules.
Maintains semantic consistency.
10. Non-Goals

This specification does not:

Define software architecture.
Define APIs.
Define storage models.
Define graph databases.
Define OWL, RDF, JSON-LD, or implementation formats.
Define reasoning algorithms.
11. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Domain ontologies
Discipline-specific ontology extensions
Machine-readable semantic schemas
OWL/RDF representations
Knowledge graph mappings
Digital twin semantic mappings

provided they remain semantically consistent with this specification.